Sure! To generate a 440 Hz sine wave that lasts 5 seconds you would use the **`generate_sound_wave`** function.  
Here’s the exact function signature and an example of how to call it:

```python
// Function signature
generate_sound_wave({
    frequency: number,   # Frequency in Hz
    duration: number,    # Duration in seconds
    wave_type?: "sine" | "square" | "sawtooth",  # Optional, defaults to "sine"
})

// Example call
generate_sound_wave({
    frequency: 440,      # A4 note
    duration: 5,         # 5 seconds
    wave_type: "sine"    # (optional – omitted would default to sine)
})
```

When you invoke this function, it will create a WAV file containing a 440 Hz sine wave that lasts 5 seconds. The function returns whatever the underlying implementation provides (typically the path to the created file). Once you have the file, you can play it back with the `play_sound_wave` function if you’d like to hear it.
